00:58:35,555 --> 00:58:41,555
The Big Red One is a 1980 American epic war film written and directed by Samuel Fuller, and starring Lee Marvin alongside an ensemble
supporting cast, including Mark Hamill, Robert Carradine, Bobby Di Cicco, Kelly Ward, Siegfried Rauch and Stéphane Audran.

15
01:02:46,666 --> 01:02:52,666
The film was based on Fuller's own experiences as a U.S.

16
01:06:57,777 --> 01:07:03,777
Army soldier during World War II.

17
01:11:08,888 --> 01:11:14,888
The title refers to the nickname of the 1st
Infantry Division, Fuller's wartime unit.

18
01:15:20,000 --> 01:15:26,000
A longtime passion project of Fuller's, development of
The Big Red One began in the late 1950s.

19
01:19:31,111 --> 01:19:37,111
The film was produced independently on a low budget, and shot
on-location in Israel and Ireland as a cost-saving measure.

20
01:23:42,222 --> 01:23:48,222
During post-production, distributor United Artists recut the film
without Fuller's input, significantly shortening the runtime.

21
01:27:53,333 --> 01:27:59,333
The film premiered at the 1980 Cannes Film Festival,
where it was nominated for the Palme d'Or.

22
01:32:04,444 --> 01:32:10,444
It was theatrically released in the United States on
July 13, 1980 to positive reviews.

23
01:36:15,555 --> 01:36:21,555
Fuller also published a book with the same title, which was more a companion novel than a
novelization of the film, although it features many of the scenes that were originally cut.

24
01:40:26,666 --> 01:40:32,666
A restored version closer to Fuller's original vision, The Big Red One: The Reconstruction,
premièred at the 2004 Cannes Film Festival, seven years after Fuller's death.

25
01:44:37,777 --> 01:44:43,777
The film explores themes of sicily, italy, africa,
concentration camp, world war ii, omaha beach.

26
01:48:48,888 --> 01:48:54,888
The tone is emotional.
